<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-sine-cosine-graphs" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Graphs of Sine and Cosine</title>
<definition xml:id="def-graph-sine">
    <title>Graph of the Sine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The equation <m>y=\sin(x)</m> is found by plotting the points <m>(x,y)=(\theta,\sin(\theta))</m> where <m>x</m> is measured in radians.</p>
            <p>
            Instead of a circle, this graph is a wave that oscillates between <m>y=-1</m> and <m>y=+1</m>, and this wave pattern repeats every <m>2\pi</m> units on the <m>x</m>-axis. Unless more of the graph is needed, we typically only draw one period of the graph.
        </p>
         <table xml:id="table-sine-values-for-graph">
    <title>Points on the graph of <m>y=\sin(x)</m>.</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" top="major">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{2\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{3\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{5\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{7\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{5\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{4\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{5\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{7\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{11\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
<figure xml:id="fig-sine-graph-base">
    <caption>A blank graph of the coordinate plane where the <m>x</m>-axis is labeled every <m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m> units and the <m>y</m>-axis is labeled every <m>0.2</m> units. A dashed grid outlines <m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m> by <m>0.2</m> rectangles.</caption>
    <image source="trigonometry/sine-cosine-blank.png" decorative="yes"/>
</figure>
    </statement>
</definition>
<definition xml:id="def-graph-cosine">
    <title>Graph of the Cosine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The equation <m>y=\cos(x)</m> is found by plotting the points <m>(x,y)=(\theta,\cos(\theta))</m> where <m>x</m> is measured in radians.</p>
            <p>
            Similar to the sine graph, the graph of cosine is a wave that oscillates between <m>y=-1</m> and <m>y=+1</m>, and this wave pattern also repeats every <m>2\pi</m> units on the <m>x</m>-axis. Furthermore, if we consider the cofunction identity <me>\cos(x) = \sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2} - x\right)</me>, we see that the graph of cosine is a transformation of the sine graph by performing a horizontal reflection and then a shift right by <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>.
        </p>
         <table xml:id="table-cosine-values-for-graph">
    <title>Points on the graph of <m>y=\cos(x)</m>.</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" top="major">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{2\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{3\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{5\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{7\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{5\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{4\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{5\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{7\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{11\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
<figure xml:id="fig-cosine-graph-base">
    <caption>A blank graph of the coordinate plane where the <m>x</m>-axis is labeled every <m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m> units and the <m>y</m>-axis is labeled every <m>0.2</m> units. A dashed grid outlines <m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m> by <m>0.2</m> rectangles.</caption>
    <image source="trigonometry/sine-cosine-blank.png" decorative="yes"/>
</figure>
    </statement>
</definition>
<definition xml:id="def-amplitude">
    <title>The Amplitude of Sine and Cosine Graphs</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>y=A\sin(x)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(x)</m> where <m>A\ne 0</m>. The <term>amplitude</term> of the graph is one-half the vertical distance between the absolute maximum and the absolute minimum of the graph.  
            The distance measured by the amplitude is given by the formula <me>\text{amplitude} = |A|.</me>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-amplitude-sine">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=A\sin(x)</m>. 
                    The point <m>(\pi/2,A)</m> is labeled as the absolute maximum and the point <m>(3\pi/2,-A)</m> is labeled as the absolute minimum.
                    One vertical line segment measures the distance between the <m>x</m>-axis and the absolute maximum, and another vertical line segment measures the distance between the <m>x</m>-axis and the absolute minimum. 
                    Both segments are labeled with the distance <m>|A|</m>.
                </caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-sine.png" decorative="yes" width="65%"/>
            </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-amplitude-cosine">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=A\cos(x)</m>. 
                    The point <m>(0,A)</m> is labeled as the absolute maximum and the point <m>(\pi,-A)</m> is labeled as the absolute minimum.
                    One vertical line segment measures the distance between the <m>x</m>-axis and the absolute maximum, and another vertical line segment measures the distance between the <m>x</m>-axis and the absolute minimum. 
                    Both segments are labeled with the distance <m>|A|</m>.
                </caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-cosine.png" decorative="yes" width="65%"/> 
            </figure>
        </statement>
</definition>
<note>
    <title>Algebraic Interpretation of Amplitude</title>
        <p>
            The amplitude measure the vertical stretch or compression applied to the base graph <m>y=\sin(x)</m> or <m>y=\cos(x)</m>.
            In order to graph <m>y=A\sin(x)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(x)</m>, we can first graph the base graph and multiply the <m>y</m>-coordinates by <m>A</m>. </p><p>
            Notice we didn't apply absolute values to this multiplication.
            If <m>A</m> is negative, multiplying by <m>A</m> will also reflect the graph across the <m>x</m>-axis in addition to stretching or compressing the graph.
        </p>
</note>
<lemma xml:id="lem-rapid-sketch-method-version-1">
    <title>Rapid Sketch Method (Amplitude Only Version)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            In order to rapidly sketch the graph of <m>y=A\sin(x)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(x)</m>, we only graph the absolute maximums, the absolute minimums, and the zeroes of the function.
            For both functions, these points correspond to the <m>x</m>-coordinates that are multiples of <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>y=A\sin(x)</m>, the pattern of points for one period (starting at <m>x=0</m>) is:
            <md>
                <mrow>(0,0)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2},A\right)\amp\amp(\pi,0)\amp\amp\left(\frac{3\pi}{2},-A\right)\amp\amp(2\pi,0)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>y=A\cos(x)</m>, the pattern of points for one period (starting at <m>x=0</m>) is:
            <md>
                <mrow>\left(0,A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2},0\right)\amp\amp(\pi,-A)\amp\amp\left(\frac{3\pi}{2},0\right)\amp\amp(2\pi,A)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<example xml:id="exa-graphing-sine-1">
    <title>Graphing a Sine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Graph one period of the function <m>y=3\sin(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="amplitude-sine-example">
        <caption>The graph of <m>y=\sin(x)</m> is drawn as a red, dashed curve on <m>\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{7\pi}{2}\right]\times[-1.25,1.25]</m>. The graph is labeled at <m>y=\sin(x)</m>, and a graph, dashed grid is imposed on the backgound; each square of the grid has dimensions <m>\frac{\pi}{4}\times 0.2</m>.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-sine-example.png" decorative="yes" width="75%"/>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-graphing-cosine-1">
    <title>Graphing a Cosine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Graph one period of the function <m>y=-\dfrac{2}{5}\cos(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="amplitude-cosine-example">
        <caption>The graph of <m>y=\cos(x)</m> is drawn as a red, dashed curve on <m>\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{7\pi}{2}\right]\times[-0.75,0.75]</m>. The graph is labeled at <m>y=\cos(x)</m>, and a graph, dashed grid is imposed on the backgound; each square of the grid has dimensions <m>\frac{\pi}{4}\times 0.2</m>.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-cosine-example.png" decorative="yes" width="60%"/>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-find-equation-graph-sine-cosine">
    <title>Finding the Equation from the Graph of a Sine or Cosine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find the equation of the sine or cosine graph shown below.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="find-equation-graph-sine-cosine">
        <caption>A graph of either a sine or cosine function.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/find-equation-sine-cosine-amplitude.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
                Graph passing through the points (0,0), (pi/2,5/4), (pi,0), (3pi/2,-5/4), and (2pi,0).
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>
<definition xml:id="def-period-sine-cosine">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>y=A\sin(Bx)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(Bx)</m> where <m>A\ne 0</m> and <m>B\gt 0</m>.</p><p>The <term>period</term> of the graph is the horizontal distance between consecutive absolute maximums (or equivalently between consecutive absolute minimums) of the graph.  
            The length of this horizontal distance is given by the following formula:
            <md>\text{period} = \dfrac{2\pi}{B}.</md>
        </p>
        
        <figure xml:id="fig-period-sine">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=A\sin(Bx)</m>. 
                    Line segments measuring the horizontal distance between absolute extrema have been added to the graph of Figure <xref ref="fig-amplitude-sine"/>.
                    Each segment is labeled with the distance <m>\frac{2\pi}{B}</m>.
                </caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-sine.png" decorative="yes" width="65%"/>
            </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-period-cosine">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=A\cos(Bx)</m>. 
                    Line segments measuring the horizontal distance between absolute extrema have been added to the graph of Figure <xref ref="fig-amplitude-cosine"/>.
                    Each segment is labeled with the distance <m>\frac{2\pi}{B}</m>.
                </caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-cosine.png" decorative="yes" width="65%"/> 
            </figure>
    </statement>
</definition>
<lemma xml:id="lem-rapid-sketch-method-version-2">
    <title>Rapid Sketch Method (Amplitude and Period Only Version)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            In order to rapidly sketch the graph of <m>y=A\sin(Bx)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(Bx)</m>, we only graph the absolute maximums, the absolute minimums, and the zeroes of the function.
            For both functions, these points correspond to the <m>x</m>-coordinates that are multiples of <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2B}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>y=A\sin(Bx)</m>, the pattern of points for one period (starting at <m>x=0</m>) is:
            <md>
                <mrow>(0,0)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2B},A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{\pi}{B},0\right)\amp\amp\left(\frac{3\pi}{2B},-A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{B},0\right)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>y=A\cos(Bx)</m>, the pattern of points for one period (starting at <m>x=0</m>) is:
            <md>
                <mrow>\left(0,A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2B},0\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{\pi}{B},-A\right)\amp\amp\left(\frac{3\pi}{2B},0\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{B},A\right)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<example xml:id="exa-graphing-sine-2">
    <title>Graphing a Sine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Graph one period of the function <m>y=\frac{3}{2}\sin\left(3x\right)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="amplitude-period-sine-example">
        <caption>The graph of <m>y=\sin(x)</m> is drawn as a red, dashed curve on <m>\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{5\pi}{2}\right]\times[-2.5,2.5]</m>.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-sine-example.png" decorative="yes" width="75%"/>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-graphing-cosine-2">
    <title>Graphing a Cosine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Graph one period of the function <m>y=-2\cos\left(\dfrac{1}{2}x\right)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="amplitude-period-cosine-example">
        <caption>The graph of <m>y=\cos(x)</m> is drawn as a red, dashed curve on <m>\left[-\pi,5\pi\right]\times[-2.5,2.5]</m>.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-cosine-example.png" decorative="yes" width="60%"/>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-graphing-sine-3">
    <title>Graphing a Sine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Graph one period of the function <m>y=-\dfrac{1}{2}\sin\left(\pi x\right)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="amplitude-period-sine-example-2">
        <caption>The graph of <m>y=\sin(x)</m> is drawn as a red, dashed curve on <m>\left[-2,8\right]\times[-1.5,1.5]</m>.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-sine-example-2.png" decorative="yes" width="60%"/>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="exa-find-equation-graph-sine-cosine-2">
    <title>Finding the Equation from the Graph of a Sine or Cosine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find the equation of the sine or cosine graph shown below.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="find-equation-graph-sine-cosine-2">
        <caption>A graph of either a sine or cosine function.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/find-equation-sine-cosine-amplitude-period.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
                Graph passing through the points (0,-1), (2,0), (4,1), (6,0)), and (8,-1).
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>
    </statement>
</example>
<definition xml:id="def-phase-shift-sine-cosine">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>y=A\sin(Bx-C)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(Bx-C)</m> where <m>A\ne 0</m>, <m>B\gt 0</m>, and <m>C</m> is any real number.</p><p>The <term>phase shift</term> of the graph is the horizontal distance the graph of <m>y=A\sin(Bx)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(Bx)</m> is shifted left or right.  
            The length of the shift is given by the following equation:
            <me>\text{phase shift} = \frac{C}{B}.</me>
            If the phase shift is positive, the graph is shifted to the right, and if the phase shift is negative, the graph is shifted to the left.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-amplitude-period-phase-shift-sine">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=A\sin(Bx-C)</m>. 
                    The graph of Figure <xref ref="fig-period-sine"/> has been shifted to the right by <m>\frac{C}{B}</m> units, where <m>C\gt 0</m>.
                    A horizontal line segment measuring the distance of the phase shift has been added to the graph and is labeled with the distance <m>\frac{C}{B}</m>.
                </caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-phase-shift-sine.png" decorative="yes" width="65%"/>
            </figure>
    </statement>
</definition>

<lemma xml:id="lem-rapid-sketch-method-version-3">
    <title>Rapid Sketch Method (Amplitude, Period, Phase Shift Only Version)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            In order to rapidly sketch the graph of <m>y=A\sin(Bx-C)</m> or <m>y=A\cos(Bx-C)</m>, we only graph the absolute maximums, the absolute minimums, and the zeroes of the function.
            For both functions, these points correspond to the <m>x</m>-coordinates that are equal to <m>\dfrac{C}{B}</m> plus a multiple of <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2B}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>y=A\sin(Bx-C)</m>, the pattern of points for one period (starting at <m>x=\dfrac{C}{B}</m>) is:
            <md>
                <mrow>\left(\dfrac{C}{B},0\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\dfrac{\pi}{2B},A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\dfrac{\pi}{B},0\right)</mrow>
                <mrow>\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\frac{3\pi}{2B},-A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\dfrac{2\pi}{B},0\right)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>y=A\cos(Bx-C)</m>, the pattern of points for one period (starting at <m>x=\dfrac{C}{B}</m>) is:
            <md>
                <mrow>\left(\dfrac{C}{B},A\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\dfrac{\pi}{2B},0\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\dfrac{\pi}{B},-A\right)</mrow>
                <mrow>\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\frac{3\pi}{2B},0\right)\amp\amp\left(\dfrac{C}{B}+\dfrac{2\pi}{B},A\right)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<example>
    <title>Graphing a Cosine Function</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Graph one period of the function <m>y=2\cos\left(3x+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right).</m>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="amplitude-period-phase-shift-cosine-example">
        <caption>The graph of <m>y=\cos(x)</m> is drawn as a red, dashed curve on <m>[-\pi,5\pi]\times[-2.5,2.5]</m>.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/amplitude-period-phase-shift-cosine-example.png" decorative="yes" width="75%"/>
    </figure>
</statement>
</example>
</worksheet>